dence":90,"evidenceIds":["E12","E2"]},{"itemId":"I23","answer":"sufficient","confidence":80,"evidenceIds":["E8","E9","E14","E15"]}],"evidenceRequests":["Q3","Q2","Q1"],"summary":"The on-time decline is real: independent ride checks match AVL, and ridership and dwell time are flat. It stems from two operational causes. First, recovery time was cut on 21 routes. Late first-timepoint departures rose on those routes, and the unchanged routes show no decline. Second, lane reductions on five corridor routes, where running times doubled in the work zones, caused a further step down on those routes. The 40% absence rise is mostly a recording artifact. ShiftLine began counting partial-day occurrences, which account for 238 of the 245 monthly increase. Full-day absences rose only 1.1%, and the dispatch log shows no change in unoperated trips or pull-out lateness. The proposed discipline program would punish operators for a measurement change and for schedule-driven early leaves. Restore recovery time, set construction running times, and restate the absence data first."}}